nd being able to play  such a deep profound character as  general Karn absolutely astounding for  me with more than 1 million dollars in  the bank for Axanar between its  Kickstarter and IndieGoGo campaigns  Peters and his crew began construction  on a full-fledged set and soundstage  meanwhile CBS and paramount sprung into  action I wake up in the morning I opened  my computer I look at Facebook and I see  this article on Hollywood Reporter and  it says CBS and paramount sue Star Trek  fan film then I realize oh that's  probably us CBS had started a war the  lawsuit alleged copyright infringement  and demanded Axanar put an immediate  stop to production they also faced fines  of up to $150,000 per violation CBS  declined to participate in this video  but referred us to an interview that  their senior vice-president of licensing  gave to the official Star Trek podcast  the production started spiraling larger  and a larger there's something of an  arms race about how many Hollywood names  could be attached how many people that  have previously worked on track how many  famous actors could you involve and  that's not really in the spirit of fan  fiction there's a lot of fans who are  gifted professionals and I say  professionals they have professional  skills technology  has advanced way beyond what we're used  to now there's the democratization of  filmmaking and then you have  crowdfunding and the studio's don't know  how to react to it like big corporations  they react by rejecting it rather than  figuring out how to embrace it I don't  think any of this is happening in a  vacuum so I think along with Alec Peters  creating a studio and with racks not  raising a million dollars there was also  the 50th anniversary there was the new  movie coming out there was the new TV  series coming out there was probably a  desire by the studio let's concentrate  on our Star Trek and not let fans have  their Star Trek for a bit people have  gotten used to full like 1960's length 